  "query_preview": "..It has been a relatively quiet overnight period as\nof 2 AM with just a few isolated light showers and mostly clear\nskies for many areas. High pressure is centered over Colorado this\nmorning and is forecast to start sliding southeast toward TX/OK by\nTuesday and Wednesday. Much above normal precipitable water values\nwill remain over our area over the next several days. A weak upper\nlevel disturbance near El Paso will move toward southeast AZ later\ntoday and then up into central/northern AZ Tuesday. This feature\nshould enhance storm coverage, as well as introduce some higher\nwind shear values and increase the threat of hail with storms in\naddition to the continued flash flood risk. A few storms this\nafternoon into Tuesday may produce hail up to 1\" diameter. As the\ndisturbance moves into the area Tuesday, the flash flood threat\nmay increase enough to warrant a Flash Flood Watch - we will\ncontinue to monitor. Active monsoon conditions look to continue\nWednesday and Thursday, then much of the guidance moves the high\npressure center westward toward Arizona and strengthens it. While\nthis would not end storm chances, the warmer air aloft may reduce\ncoverage of the activity and this is reflected in our latest\nforecasts. PoP values lower to 30-50% over the higher terrain and\nunder 30% for lower elevations Friday through Sunday. With the\nridge moving closer, daytime temperatures will slowly rise to\nabove normal toward the end of the week/weekend.",
